#open-question(title: [Peripheral Muscle Failure or Reduced Central Drive?])[
A lower grip force and a higher fatigue ratio are compatible with at least two mechanisms that the current data cannot separate.

*Peripheral interpretation.* Repeated maximal contractions progressively deplete the muscle's own capacity. In ME/CFS, this reading is supported by the stable group separation on fatigue and recovery ratios @Jakel2021HGS @Mihatsch2026HandgripCYP and by the association of HGS with severity scores @Wallraven2026HandgripPCS.

*Central interpretation.* The muscle behaves normally and the deficit lies in drive to the muscle. In 1993, sustained maximal dorsiflexion in CFS patients showed *similar* peripheral fatigue and metabolic response to controls, but significantly lower voluntary activation — an inability to activate muscle fully during intense sustained exercise @KentBraun1993central. (The cohort is pre-CCC "CFS" and the muscle studied was the tibialis anterior, not a hand muscle; the finding is about central activation as a construct, not about grip specifically.)

*A third reading, which is not a disease mechanism.* Within-subject variability across repeated maximal grips is about 2.5 times higher in ME/CFS than in controls (coefficient of variation 14.7% vs 5.9%), above a 15% threshold previously proposed to mark submaximal effort @Popkirov2025CV. This describes inconsistent force production; it does not identify a tissue or a drive defect, and it must not be converted into a judgment about whether a patient is trying (see @pwarn:handgrip-effort-judgment).

Independent support for the central reading comes from a separate multimodal imaging study in which patients developed fatigue earlier during a sustained grip task *despite equivalent maximum voluntary force*, with minimal increase in motor-cortical and subcortical output as the task progressed @Bedard2026centralFatigability (see @clf:central-fatigability). That is the opposite pattern to a muscle that simply cannot produce force.

The central reading is itself disputed. A published comment argues that the coefficient of variation cannot determine whether an effort is sincere and reports that the stated participant numbers could not be reproduced from the public dataset @Steinkirchner2026comment. The disagreement is about method, not about the existence of the variability.

*Certainty: 0.47–0.60* (discounted; the two mechanisms are equally well evidenced, with comparable designs). A useful next step does not require a new patient cohort: the ten-grip force series can be decomposed into a monotonic drift (fatigue) and residual noise (force variability). If drift and noise behave as separate signals — drift tracking symptom severity, noise tracking time of day or motivation — then "fatigue" and "unreliable effort" are not competing explanations of one number but two measurable components of the same trace. If they covary tightly, the decomposition adds nothing and the variability argument collapses back into the peripheral-versus-central dispute.

*Prediction:* voluntary activation will fall across sessions in ME/CFS while peripheral twitch force is preserved. *Falsified if:* peripheral twitch force declines disproportionately with normal activation — the peripheral arm would then be supported instead.

The decisive experiment is a twitch-interpolation or motor-cortex-stimulation study performed *during* the standard two-session grip protocol: if voluntary activation falls across sessions while peripheral twitch force is preserved, the deficit is central. If peripheral twitch force declines disproportionately, it is muscular. The distinction matters clinically: a central deficit is a brain-drive measurement, a peripheral deficit is a muscle measurement, and they imply different research and treatment directions.

*Consequence:* Until this is resolved, handgrip results should be reported as "performance deficit, mechanism undetermined" — not as evidence of muscle disease and not as evidence of insufficient effort.
] <oq:handgrip-peripheral-vs-central>
